WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.560
Nine to eleven litres of fluid enter your gut each day.

2
00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:06.740
A couple of hundred grams of stool leave.

3
00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:11.180
Where did that water go, and what decides how much leaves?

4
00:00:14.717 --> 00:00:16.777
About 2 litres is what you drink.

5
00:00:18.097 --> 00:00:24.797
Your own secretions add roughly 7 to 9 more: saliva, gastric juice, bile, pancreatic

6
00:00:24.797 --> 00:00:26.277
juice and intestinal fluid.

7
00:00:27.617 --> 00:00:29.757
About 99 percent is taken back.

8
00:00:30.767 --> 00:00:33.867
So what leaves is whatever was not taken back.

9
00:00:35.087 --> 00:00:39.107
Stool water = load × (1 − fraction reabsorbed).

10
00:00:40.107 --> 00:00:45.467
With round numbers, 11 litres × 1 percent is about a tenth of a liter a day.

11
00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:52.810
Try two changes. Add 1 litre to the load, and keep 99 percent capture.

12
00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:56.610
Or keep the load, and capture 1 point less.

13
00:00:57.910 --> 00:00:59.350
Which raises stool water more?

14
00:01:05.283 --> 00:01:10.463
The extra litre adds only about a hundredth of a litre, in proportion to the load.

15
00:01:11.743 --> 00:01:16.603
One point less capture takes stool water from 0.11 litres to 0.22.

16
00:01:17.923 --> 00:01:19.203
It doubles.

17
00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:21.167
Here is the one idea.

18
00:01:22.547 --> 00:01:24.927
What leaves is a small residue of a large flow.

19
00:01:26.147 --> 00:01:30.487
When capture is nearly complete, that residue is very sensitive to it.

20
00:01:31.587 --> 00:01:33.967
A bigger load raises it only in proportion.

21
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.320
Who adds to the load, and who captures?

22
00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:44.040
In pits called crypts, a sodium pump and NKCC1 pack chloride into the cell.

23
00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:49.180
When the channel CFTR opens, chloride pours into the gut.

24
00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:53.700
Sodium follows between the cells, and water follows both.

25
00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:58.520
On the fingers called villi, the flow runs the other way.

26
00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:03.720
NHE3 and a partner exchanger bring sodium chloride in.

27
00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:07.700
SGLT1 carries sodium in with sugar.

28
00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:10.600
Water follows the absorbed salt.

29
00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:16.770
Secretion mostly happens in the crypts, and absorption mostly on the villi, though

30
00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:17.950
one cell can do both.

31
00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:20.670
Stool water is the difference.

32
00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:26.690
Behind them stands the colon, which can absorb up to 4 to 5 litres a day.

33
00:02:27.783 --> 00:02:30.063
Cholera pulls on both terms at once.

34
00:02:31.303 --> 00:02:36.663
Its toxin jams a G protein in the on position, so cells keep making cyclic AMP.

35
00:02:38.123 --> 00:02:41.523
In the crypts, that opens CFTR, and the load rises.

36
00:02:42.923 --> 00:02:46.303
On the villi, it slows NHE3, and capture falls.

37
00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:49.227
So the gut works near an edge.

38
00:02:50.427 --> 00:02:52.027
It takes back nearly everything.

39
00:02:53.287 --> 00:02:57.967
So a small slip in any capturing step shows up many times over in what leaves.
